# core.autocrlf=true is set at system scope on the Windows boxes that build this
# repo, so a plain checkout writes CRLF into every file git touches, and a gate that
# matches on the TEXT of a source file then matches nothing at all. That is not a gate
# failing; it is a gate reporting the clean run of a complete gate having read nothing,
# which is the most expensive failure mode this tree has. It has already happened here
# once, to 84 source anchors that passed vacuously on Windows. Every repair to these
# packages is made as an exact-string edit over source, and an edit anchored on one
# ending matches nothing in a file using the other: it edits nothing, the suite then
# passes, and that reads as "the change was made and was harmless".
#
# mls/vectors_runner_test.go holds the same property as a gate, over every package of the
# module: it walks the module root, judges every directory holding Go source, and resolves
# the ending each file is required to carry out of THIS file. It used to derive that scope by
# following "../" path literals out of mls, which reached connect/message and
# connect/messagegroup and could not reach a CHILD directory at all; connect/mls/syntax sat in
# exactly that blind spot at 6 CRLF against 17 LF, and connect/message imports it. That
# closure is gone as of 2026-09-05 and the scope is the module.
#
# The pin still reaches what no scan can: a file this working tree has not got, in a checkout
# nobody has made yet. The gate reaches what the pin cannot: what the working tree actually
# carries right now, which is the only place the drift is ever visible.
#
# It costs no git object. autocrlf cleans on the way in, so all 474 .go blobs were
# already LF when this line was added; what it rewrites is the working tree, which is the
# only place the drift was ever visible and the only place an anchored edit reads.
#
# msgrepo pins its Go the same way and names this same failure mode.
#
# *.pb.go below is SUBSUMED by this line rather than in conflict with it -- both say
# eol=lf -- and it stays because the comment under it argues regenerate-and-diff, which is
# about *.proto as much as about the generated Go.
#
# EOL=LF ACTS ONLY AT CHECKOUT, and that single fact explains the whole episode. All seven
# *.pb.go files were already pinned by the rule below, and on 2026-09-05, before the
# renormalisation that landed with this line, five of them were still CRLF on disk anyway: a
# pin added after a file has been checked out does not go back and rewrite it. This file
# governs what the NEXT checkout writes. The working tree is governed by whatever last wrote
# it, which on this project is as often a tool as it is git.
#
# So a pin is never on its own evidence that a tree is clean, and that is why it is not left
# on its own. mls/vectors_runner_test.go's TestThePackageSourceIsOneLineEndingThroughout READS
# this file, derives lf from the line below rather than hard-coding it, and holds the working
# tree of every package in this module to it. Delete that line and
# every file it judges reports as pinned to nothing and the gate goes red -- which is the point
# of deriving it: the pin and the gate are one mechanism and neither half was worth anything
# alone. Until 2026-09-05 they were two, and deleting this line was invisible to all 7,495
# tests in the suite.
*.go text eol=lf

# protoc emits LF and nothing downstream normalizes it back, so a checkout that
# writes CRLF makes every regeneration rewrite every line ending. `git status`
# then reports the generated files as modified while `git diff` reports nothing,
# and the reproducibility check "regenerate and see an empty diff" stops meaning
# what it says. core.autocrlf=true is set at system scope on the Windows boxes
# that build this repo, which is exactly that checkout.
#
# Pinning the protobuf inputs and their generated Go to LF keeps the regenerate-
# and-diff check honest: after this, a diff in a .pb.go is a real change.
*.proto text eol=lf
*.pb.go text eol=lf

# The committed fuzz seed corpus is bytes, not text. core.autocrlf=true is set at
# system scope on the Windows boxes that build this repo, and git decides text
# versus binary by looking for a NUL octet in the first 8000: a seed that happens
# to hold no NUL and does hold an 0x0a is rewritten on checkout, which silently
# turns a valid encoding into one no decoder accepts. Marking the folder binary is
# the only thing between "the corpus is evidence" and "the corpus is whatever the
# checkout left behind".
mls/testdata/corpus/** -text
